    "Requirement Analysis": "Architecture plan, files, and tasks (roles: Bob, Eve, Alex). Sizing target: about 9 repository files, ~38 functions/methods, ~776-820 LOC. Preserve protocol-required files exactly.\n\nFile list (default package, dependency order):\n1) pom.xml (owner: Eve)\n   - Task: Configure Maven for Java 11. Declare org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter with test scope and a Maven Surefire plugin version that runs JUnit 5. No external non-test dependencies. Repeat the exact dependency contract and ensure mvn -q test runs JUnit Jupiter.\n   - Allowed import direction: build only; no code dependencies.\n\n2) src/main/java/Player.java (owner: Bob)\n   - Task: Implement an enum Player with constants X and O. Methods: Player next() returning the other player; toString() stable. No package declaration.\n   - API semantics: Player.next() is used to toggle turns only while IN_PROGRESS; after a win the currentPlayer remains the winner.\n\n3) src/main/java/Move.java (owner: Bob)\n   - Task: Implement an immutable class Move with fields: int row, int col, Player player, int index (0-based move count). Provide getters, equals/hashCode, toString. Constructor validates non-negative index and valid player reference; row/col may be any integer (board bounds enforced elsewhere).\n   - Semantics: Every successful placeMove appends exactly one Move to history.\n\n4) src/main/java/GameState.java (owner: Eve)\n   - Task: Implement a value type GameState with enum Status { IN_PROGRESS, DRAW, X_WON, O_WON }. Provide Optional<Player> getWinner(). Static constructors: inProgress(), draw(), won(Player p) mapping Player.X->X_WON and Player.O->O_WON, winner empty for inProgress/draw. equals/hashCode.\n   - Semantics: After a win, currentPlayer remains the winner; draw has empty winner.\n\n5) src/main/java/Board.java (owner: Alex)\n   - Task: Implement Board with a square grid int size; backing 2D array of Player or null for empty-cell representation. Methods:\n     - Board(int size): validate size>=5 else throw IllegalArgumentException.\n     - int getSize()\n     - boolean isInBounds(int row, int col)\n     - Player getCell(int row, int col)\n     - boolean isEmpty(int row, int col): returns true iff getCell==null; this is the single empty-cell representation.\n     - boolean setCell(int row, int col, Player p): set only if cell empty and in bounds; return true; otherwise false with no mutation (Game guards additional rules).\n     - void clearCell(int row, int col): set cell to null; must agree with isEmpty.\n     - Player[][] snapshot(): deep copy of the grid.\n   - Semantics: Board state is the source of truth; empty-cell representation is null and is consistent across isEmpty and clearCell.\n\n6) src/main/java/WinDetector.java (owner: Alex)\n   - Task: Implement a pure utility class WinDetector with static boolean hasFiveInRow(Board b, int row, int col, Player p). The function must count the just-played stone plus matching stones in both signs of exactly the four directions: (0,1) horizontal, (1,0) vertical, (1,1) diagonal down-right, (1,-1) diagonal up-right. If any direction count >= 5, return true; otherwise false. Only inspect board with b.getCell and b.isInBounds; do not mutate.\n   - Semantics: five or more contiguous stones wins immediately.\n\n7) src/main/java/Game.java (owner: Bob)\n   - Task: Implement the canonical Game API exactly:\n     - public Game(int size)\n     - public Board getBoard()\n     - public Player getCurrentPlayer()\n     - public GameState getState()\n     - public boolean placeMove(int row, int col)\n     - public boolean undo()\n     - public void reset()\n     - public java.util.List<Move> getHistory()\n   - Construction: Game(int size) rejects size<5 with IllegalArgumentException; initialize Board(size), currentPlayer=Player.X, state=GameState.inProgress(), empty history.\n   - placeMove semantics: while IN_PROGRESS, return false without mutation for out-of-bounds or occupied cell. Otherwise, set board cell with currentPlayer, append exactly one Move with index=history.size(), then evaluate win via WinDetector.hasFiveInRow(board,row,col,currentPlayer); if win, state=GameState.won(currentPlayer) and currentPlayer remains the winner; else if board is full, state=GameState.draw(); else state remains IN_PROGRESS and advance currentPlayer=currentPlayer.next(). If state is terminal (DRAW/X_WON/O_WON), placeMove returns false without mutation.\n   - undo semantics: undo returns false without mutation when history is empty; otherwise remove the latest move, clear that board cell, restore currentPlayer to the removed move's player, restore state=GameState.inProgress(), and return true.\n   - reset semantics: clear every board cell and the complete history, restore currentPlayer=Player.X and state=GameState.inProgress(); it must never derive the first player from the pre-reset current turn.\n   - getHistory: return an unmodifiable copy or view to prevent external mutation.\n   - Allowed import direction: depends on Player, Move, Board, GameState, WinDetector.\n\n8) src/main/java/Main.java (owner: Eve)\n   - Task: Runtime entry. Provide public class Main with public static void main(String[] args). Create a small demonstration: construct new Game(15), place a few safe moves (e.g., one or two valid moves including placeMove(1,1) then ensure turn toggles), print one concise line like \"Caro core ready\" or a single status print, and return normally. No System.exit, no loops, no input, no GUI, no threads.\n   - Semantics: Main.main must finish by normal method return after at most one concise print.\n\n9) src/test/java/MainTest.java (owner: Alex)\n   - Task: Provide exactly three JUnit Jupiter tests:\n     - Test 1: Main.main(new String[0]) returns normally.\n     - Test 2: The exact horizontal win sequence on one new Game(15): execute (7,3),(0,0),(7,4),(0,2),(7,5),(0,4),(7,6),(0,6),(7,7); assert all nine placeMove calls are true; then assert status X_WON, winner Optional.of(Player.X), currentPlayer Player.X, history size 9; do not construct a second game; do not loop; do not directly call WinDetector; and never assert that the ninth move is false; after win perform exactly one terminal placeMove attempt anywhere and assert false with no mutation.\n     - Test 3: The state fixture: new Game(15) starts X; placeMove(1,1) is true and changes turn to O; repeated placeMove(1,1) and placeMove(-1,0) are false and leave turn O/history size 1; placeMove(2,2) is true and changes turn to X; undo is true, makes (2,2) empty, restores turn O, leaves history size 1 and IN_PROGRESS; reset restores X, empty (1,1), empty history, and IN_PROGRESS; never expect O immediately after reset.\n   - Testing constraints: keep concise, deterministic, and under ~150 LOC; import only canonical public API.\n\nFull API specification (explicit repetition, protocol-critical):\n- Game(int size) rejects size<5 with IllegalArgumentException and every new or reset game starts with Player.X.\n- Board getBoard(), Player getCurrentPlayer(), GameState getState(), boolean placeMove(int row,int col), boolean undo(), void reset(), java.util.List<Move> getHistory().\n- placeMove has no Player argument: while IN_PROGRESS it returns false without mutation for an out-of-bounds or occupied cell; otherwise it places currentPlayer, appends exactly one Move, evaluates win then draw, and advances to currentPlayer.next() only when the resulting state remains IN_PROGRESS; after a win currentPlayer therefore remains the winner, and every terminal call returns false without mutation.\n- GameState uses exactly Status.IN_PROGRESS, Status.DRAW, Status.X_WON, and Status.O_WON; GameState.won(Player.X) produces X_WON and winner X, GameState.won(Player.O) produces O_WON and winner O, while inProgress/draw have an empty winner.\n- undo returns false without mutation when history is empty; otherwise it removes exactly the latest move, clears that board cell, restores currentPlayer to the removed move's player, restores GameState.inProgress(), and returns true.\n- reset clears every board cell and the complete history, restores currentPlayer=Player.X and GameState.inProgress(); it must never derive the first player from the pre-reset current turn.\n- WinDetector.hasFiveInRow(Board,int,int,Player) counts the just-played stone plus matching stones in both signs of exactly (0,1), (1,0), (1,1), and (1,-1).\n- Tests include exactly three tests and must preserve the compact sequence (7,3),(0,0),(7,4),(0,2),(7,5),(0,4),(7,6),(0,6),(7,7), and the literal state fixture mentioning both placeMove(1,1) and placeMove(2,2).\n\nLogic Analysis (explicit repetition):\n- Alternation occurs only while the game remains IN_PROGRESS; after a win, currentPlayer remains the winner.\n- Illegal moves (occupied or out-of-bounds) return false with no mutation; terminal state prevents further mutation.\n- Win detection strictly evaluates two-sided contiguous counts for four directions: (0,1), (1,0), (1,1), (1,-1).\n- Undo and reset semantics follow the exact rules above; reset always restores X first, independent of prior turn.\n- Sequence coverage targets the exact nine moves: (7,3),(0,0),(7,4),(0,2),(7,5),(0,4),(7,6),(0,6),(7,7).\n\nTest Scope Plan (explicit repetition):\n- Exactly three tests; no alternative long fixtures.\n- Test invalid moves and undo/reset using the specified literal fixture including placeMove(1,1) and placeMove(2,2).\n- Test the single horizontal win with all nine placeMove calls returning true, final X_WON, currentPlayer Player.X, winner Optional.of(Player.X), history size 9, and a single terminal placeMove false.\n- Ensure Main.main returns normally, prints at most once, and does not call System.exit.\n\nShared Knowledge (explicit repetition for team alignment: Bob, Eve, Alex):\n- Canonical Game API signatures and behaviors are fixed: Game(int size), Board getBoard(), Player getCurrentPlayer(), GameState getState(), boolean placeMove(int row,int col), boolean undo(), void reset(), java.util.List<Move> getHistory().\n- X-first/reset rule and toggle-only-while-in-progress rule are mandatory.\n- Undo semantics and four-direction two-sided count in WinDetector are mandatory.\n- The single nine-move win sequence (7,3),(0,0),(7,4),(0,2),(7,5),(0,4),(7,6),(0,6),(7,7) and the state fixture mentioning both placeMove(1,1) and placeMove(2,2) must appear in tests.\n- Main.main must return normally with at most one concise print; no System.exit."
